Day 67 - Evaluate Reverse Polish Notation

Problem Statement:
You are given an array of strings tokens representing an arithmetic expression in Reverse Polish Notation (RPN).
Evaluate the expression and return the integer result.
The valid operators are +, -, *, and /.

Code:
class Solution {
public:
    int evalRPN(vector<string>& tokens) {
        stack<int> st;

        for(auto ch : tokens) {

            if(ch == "+" || ch == "-" || ch == "*" || ch == "/") {

                int a = st.top();
                st.pop();

                int b = st.top();
                st.pop();

                if(ch == "+")
                    st.push(b + a);

                else if(ch == "-")
                    st.push(b - a);

                else if(ch == "*")
                    st.push(b * a);

                else
                    st.push(b / a);
            }
            else {
                st.push(stoi(ch));
            }
        }

        return st.top();
    }
};





Time Complexity:
O(n)

Each token is processed exactly once.

Space Complexity:
O(n)

The stack can contain up to n operands.

Approach:
This solution uses a stack to evaluate the Reverse Polish Notation expression.

Traverse every token from left to right.
If the token is a number, convert it from string to integer using stoi() and push it into the stack.
If the token is an operator, take the top two elements from the stack.
Store the first popped value in a and the second popped value in b.
Perform b operator a.
Push the result back into the stack.
At the end, the stack contains only one value, which is the final answer.

